{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "בעת ההיא הבדיל ה' את שבט הלוי", :translit "ba'et hahi hivdil Hashem et shevet ha-levi", :gloss "at that time YHWH set apart the tribe of Levi"} {:he "סמך", :translit "samakh", :gloss "juxtaposed / adjacent"} {:he "מים", :translit "mayim", :gloss "waters"} {:he "לשאת את הארון ולברך", :translit "le-se'et et ha-aron u-levarekh", :gloss "to bear the Ark and to bless"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut: adjacent verses cited to infer that Levites restored Israel to proper conduct"} {:kind :other, :note "semikhut: word 'mayim' in preceding verse placed adjacent to 'to bear the Ark and to bless' to derive hand-washing before priestly blessing"}], :frame {:text "The hermeneutical principle of semikhut parshiyot — deriving law or meaning from the juxtaposition of adjacent verses — is a recognized midrashic method (Sifre Numbers 131 applies it to priestly matters). The requirement for a kohen to wash his hands before blessing the people is stated at Sukkot 44b and Shulchan Arukh Orach Chayyim 128:6. The tribe of Levi's role in bearing the Ark and blessing is described in Deuteronomy 10:8 itself.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 10:8" "Sifre Numbers 131" "Sukkah 44b" "Shulchan Arukh Orach Chayyim 128:6"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut: adjacent verses cited to infer that Levites restored Israel to proper conduct", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "semikhut: word 'mayim' in preceding verse placed adjacent to 'to bear the Ark and to bless' to derive hand-washing before priestly blessing", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:35:38.200964823Z", :model "glm-5.2"}